<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=rocket">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Transport and navigation</p><h1 class="enc-title">Rocket</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">13th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A rocket is an elongated flying vehicle driven by a rocket engine, carrying all its propellant and so needing no surrounding air; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gunpowder">gunpowder</a> rockets evolved in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song dynasty</a> by the 13th century. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> spread them to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-east">Middle East</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, and the iron-cased rockets of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mysore">Mysore</a> were the model for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congreve-rocket">Congreve rocket</a> of 1804. Modern rockets date from 1926, when Robert Goddard fitted a supersonic nozzle to a high-pressure combustion chamber, and in 1944 the German V-2 became the first artificial object to travel into space.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rocket.jpg" alt="Rocket" width="320" height="228" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: NASA · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chinese_rocket.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
